      see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/26107"
      cmd         : "/sbin/auditctl -l | /bin/grep '/usr/bin/sudo'"
      expect      : "^[\\s]*-a[\\s]+(?:always,exit|exit,always)(?:[\\s]+-S[\\s]+all)?[\\s]+-F[\\s]+path=/usr/bin/sudo[\\s]+(?:-F[\\s]+perm=[rwa]*x[rwa]*[\\s]+)?-F[\\s]+auid>=1000[\\s]+-F[\\s]+auid!=(?:4294967295|-1|unset)(?:[\\s]+(?:-k[\\s]+|-F[\\s]+key=)[\\S]+)*[\\s]*$"
    </custom_item>

    <custom_item>
      type        : CMD_EXEC
      description : "1.12 AZLX-23-000220"
      info        : "Amazon Linux 2023 must restrict usage of ptrace to descendant processes.

GROUP ID: V-274005RULE ID: SV-274005r1120003

Unrestricted usage of ptrace allows compromised binaries to run ptrace on other processes of the user. Like this, the attacker can steal sensitive information from the target processes (e.g., SSH sessions, web browser, etc.) without any additional assistance from the user (i.e., without resorting to phishing)."
      solution    : "Configure Amazon Linux 2023 to restrict usage of ptrace to descendant processes by adding the following line to a file, in the \"/etc/sysctl.d\" directory:

kernel.yama.ptrace_scope = 1

The system configuration files need to be reloaded for the changes to take effect. To reload the contents of the files, run the following command:

$ sudo sysctl --system"
      reference   : "800-171|3.13.3,800-53|SC-2,800-53r5|SC-2,CAT|II,CCI|CCI-001082,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),ITSG-33|SC-2,ITSG-33|SC-2a.,LEVEL|2A,NESA|T3.4.1,NIAv2|SS15b,Rule-ID|SV-274005r1120003_rule,STIG-ID|AZLX-23-000220,Vuln-ID|V-274005"
      see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/26107"
      cmd         : "/sbin/sysctl kernel.yama.ptrace_scope"
      expect      : "kernel\\.yama\\.ptrace_scope[\\s]*=[\\s]*1[\\s]*$"
    </custom_item>

    <custom_item>
      type        : CMD_EXEC
      description : "1.120 AZLX-23-002250"
      info        : "Amazon Linux 2023 must generate audit records for all account creations, modifications, disabling, and termination events that affect /etc/passwd.

GROUP ID: V-274113RULE ID: SV-274113r1120327

In addition to auditing new user and group accounts, these watches will alert the system administrator(s) (SAs) to any modifications. Any unexpected users, groups, or modifications should be investigated for legitimacy.

